 "raw_reply": "{\n  \"experiment_id\": \"EXP-009\",\n  \"observed_evidence\": [\n    {\"statement\": \"The selection rule is: rank by mean ROC-AUC minus 0.25×fold std, then runtime, on training CV only.\", \"citation\": \"EXP-009 / evidence.selection_rule\"},\n    {\"statement\": \"extra_trees has mean ROC-AUC 0.72884 and std 0.02954 over 3 folds with 1 repeat, at stage 'ratios', baseline optimization, empty model_params and 38 mean features. This matches the claim's 0.7288±0.0295.\", \"citation\": \"EXP-009 / evidence.model_results[1].metrics.roc_auc; evidence.model_results[1].folds; evidence.model_results[1].repeats; evidence.model_results[1].model_params\"},\n    {\"statement\": \"Other models' mean ROC-AUC ± std: logistic_regression 0.71888±0.03005, hist_gradient_boosting 0.71225±0.01468, lightgbm 0.71720±0.01650, xgboost 0.70467±0.02348.\", \"citation\": \"EXP-009 / evidence.model_results[0,2,3,4].metrics.roc_auc\"},\n    {\"statement\": \"extra_trees per-fold ROC-AUC is 0.7439, 0.7478, 0.6948. logistic_regression per-fold ROC-AUC is 0.7098, 0.7524, 0.6944.\", \"citation\": \"EXP-009 / evidence.model_results[1].fold_metrics[*].roc_auc; evidence.model_results[0].fold_metrics[*].roc_auc\"},\n    {\"statement\": \"Per-fold ROC-AUC for lightgbm is 0.7344, 0.7157, 0.7015. For hist_gradient_boosting it is 0.7252, 0.7153, 0.6963.\", \"citation\": \"EXP-009 / evidence.model_results[3].fold_metrics[*].roc_auc; evidence.model_results[2].fold_metrics[*].roc_auc\"},\n    {\"statement\": \"logistic_regression has better mean log_loss (0.3231 vs 0.3433), brier (0.0921 vs 0.0947), ece_10 (0.0238 vs 0.0414) and accuracy (0.8903 vs 0.8822) than extra_trees. Its mean avg_precision is also slightly higher (0.3401 vs 0.3356).\", \"citation\": \"EXP-009 / evidence.model_results[0].metrics.{log_loss,brier,ece_10,accuracy,avg_precision}.mean; evidence.model_results[1].metrics.{log_loss,brier,ece_10,accuracy,avg_precision}.mean\"},\n    {\"statement\": \"extra_trees mean recall at threshold 0.5 is 0.179 and mean F1 is 0.262.\", \"citation\": \"EXP-009 / evidence.model_results[1].metrics.recall.mean; evidence.model_results[1].metrics.f1.mean\"},\n    {\"statement\": \"Top extra_trees features by stability include poutcome, age, ratio_pdays_div_age, ratio_age_div_day_of_week, day_of_week and ratio_balance_div_age, each with top10_frequency 1.0. Many top features are ratios involving categorical columns such as job, month, day_of_week and poutcome.\", \"citation\": \"EXP-009 / evidence.model_results[1].feature_stability\"},\n    {\"statement\": \"hist_gradient_boosting has an empty feature_stability list.\", \"citation\": \"EXP-009 / evidence.model_results[2].feature_stability\"},\n    {\"statement\": \"The bundle-level limitations list is empty. The claim itself lists the limitation that a CV leader is not a universal winner and has not seen the locked holdout.\", \"citation\": \"EXP-009 / limitations; claims[0].limitations\"},\n    {\"statement\": \"Reported ci95 for extra_trees ROC-AUC is 0.6954–0.7623 from 3 folds.\", \"citation\": \"EXP-009 / evidence.model_results[1].metrics.roc_auc.ci95_low, ci95_high\"}\n  ],\n  \"interpretation\": \"Inference from the cited numbers: by the stated rule, extra_trees ranks first. The derived scores (mean − 0.25×std, my arithmetic) are about 0.7215 for extra_trees, 0.7131 for lightgbm, 0.7114 for logistic_regression, 0.7086 for hist_gradient_boosting and 0.6988 for xgboost. The margin over the runner-up is only about 0.008 ROC-AUC on 3 folds with 1 repeat. The paired fold differences are inconsistent in sign. Versus logistic_regression they are about +0.034, −0.005 and +0.000, so the lead rests mostly on fold 1. Versus lightgbm they are about +0.010, +0.032 and −0.007. The evidence therefore supports 'highest mean training-CV ROC-AUC under default settings in this one run', not 'strongest stable'. extra_trees' fold std (0.0295) is nearly the highest of the five models and about twice that of hist_gradient_boosting and lightgbm. The rule's weak 0.25 penalty is what lets it win. Other metrics favor logistic_regression: probability quality (log_loss, brier, ece_10), accuracy and avg_precision. A ROC-AUC rank does not establish a decision-useful or calibrated model. Recall is low at the fixed 0.5 threshold. All models use default hyperparameters, so this compares default behavior, not algorithm families. Nothing here supports causal or feature-effect conclusions. Importances are model-specific, not comparable across families, and say nothing about causal drivers. Hypothesis, not fact: the extra_trees advantage may be noise or an artifact of fold composition.\",\n  \"decision\": \"Retain extra_trees only as a provisional training-CV leading candidate, with logistic_regression and lightgbm kept as live contenders. Do not call it the stable or best algorithm family. Do not approve any feature or model for deployment, and do not touch the locked holdout until the rank is confirmed under repeated paired CV and a decision-time/production-availability review of the features is done.\",\n  \"risks_and_missing_evidence\": [\n    \"Only 3 folds and 1 repeat. The reported ci95 appears to be a normal-approximation interval (about ±1.96 SE) on 3 non-independent folds, so it understates uncertainty. It should not support rank claims.\",\n    \"No paired or significance comparison between models. Fold-level ROC-AUC ranges overlap heavily, and extra_trees' minimum fold (0.6948) is about equal to logistic_regression's minimum (0.6944).\",\n    \"All models use default parameters (model_params {}, optimization baseline), so the comparison may reflect defaults rather than family capability. Tuning could change the order, and nested CV would be needed to tune without optimistic bias.\",\n    \"Only the 'ratios' feature stage appears in the package. The claim says 'using ratios features', but there is no comparison against the raw/base stage. The utility of the ratio features is unestablished.\",\n    \"Many engineered features are ratios or log1p of categorical columns (job, month, day_of_week, poutcome, marital, loan). This implies integer or ordinal encodings, so the ratios are of arbitrary codes with questionable meaning. Encoding details are not shown.\",\n    \"Leakage risk is undocumented. It is unstated whether imputation, encoding or any target-based encoding was fit inside each training fold only. Whether a post-contact variable such as 'duration' was excluded is also unstated, since only the top-20 features are shown. Split type (stratified or not, shuffled or time-ordered) is not given.\",\n    \"Decision-time and production availability are unreviewed for the top features. poutcome, contact, pdays, previous, month and day_of_week reflect prior and current campaign contact context and need review. Timing of the day_of_week/month values relative to the prediction point must be confirmed.\",\n    \"Class prevalence and positive count per fold are not given. Recall values imply about 124–125 positives per fold (my inference), so the metrics are noisy.\",\n    \"Threshold fixed at 0.5 with low recall. There is no threshold, cost or utility analysis, and ROC-AUC alone is insufficient for any actionable use.\",\n    \"Calibration is worse for extra_trees than for logistic_regression and none of the models were calibrated. Probabilities from extra_trees should not be used as-is.\",\n    \"Feature importances come from different mechanisms per model and are missing for hist_gradient_boosting. Cross-model stability comparison is not valid, and importance is not causal.\",\n    \"Bundle-level limitations is empty, so caveats are under-reported.\"\n  ],\n  \"challenges_to_claims\": [\n    {\"claim_id\": \"EXP-009-C1\", \"issue\": \"The question asks about 'strongest stable' evidence, but extra_trees has the second-highest fold std of five models (0.0295). Its win depends on the weak 0.25×std penalty and a ~0.008 score margin. The stability framing is unsupported.\", \"severity\": \"high\"},\n    {\"claim_id\": \"EXP-009-C1\", \"issue\": \"3 folds × 1 repeat with no paired test. Per-fold advantage over logistic_regression and lightgbm changes sign, and the lead is driven largely by one fold, so the ranking is not demonstrated to be reproducible.\", \"severity\": \"high\"},\n    {\"claim_id\": \"EXP-009-C1\", \"issue\": \"The comparison covers default-parameter baselines only. It cannot speak to algorithm families in general, only to these default implementations.\", \"severity\": \"medium\"},\n    {\"claim_id\": \"EXP-009-C1\", \"issue\": \"ROC-AUC-only selection ignores that logistic_regression is better on log_loss, brier, ece_10, accuracy and mean avg_precision. 'Candidate' status is metric-dependent and should be stated.\", \"severity\": \"medium\"},\n    {\"claim_id\": \"EXP-009-C1\", \"issue\": \"Unverified leakage and production-availability status of the 'ratios' features. The claim names the feature set without decision-time review, and top features include ratios of encoded categoricals.\", \"severity\": \"high\"},\n    {\"claim_id\": \"EXP-009-C1\", \"issue\": \"The claim's 'ratios' feature set is not compared with the base feature set in this bundle, so the model candidate is conflated with feature-stage choice.\", \"severity\": \"medium\"}\n  ],\n  \"next_experiment\": {\n    \"title\": \"Repeated paired CV re-ranking of the five baselines on identical splits\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"If extra_trees' ROC-AUC lead is real rather than fold noise, it will remain rank 1 on mean − 0.25×std over repeated stratified CV with identical splits for all models, and its paired ROC-AUC difference versus each of logistic_regression and lightgbm will be positive with an interval excluding zero.\",\n    \"success_gate\": \"Using 5 repeats × 3 folds on the same training data (fixed seeds, all preprocessing and encoding fit inside each training fold, same ratios stage, default params), extra_trees ranks 1 by the EXP-009 selection rule, and the paired mean ROC-AUC difference versus both logistic_regression and lightgbm is > 0 with a repeated-CV-corrected 95% interval (e.g., Nadeau–Bengio) excluding 0. It is also reported with log_loss and ece_10 so that any calibration cost is visible. If the interval includes 0 or another model ranks 1, the 'extra_trees candidate' claim is falsified and the finding becomes 'no reliably separable leader'.\",\n    \"why_smallest\": \"It changes only the number of repeats and adds paired reporting. It reuses the existing five models, defaults and feature stage, with no tuning, no new features and no holdout access. It directly tests the weakest link, which is rank reproducibility. Documenting split type and the within-fold preprocessing check comes free with the rerun.\"\n  },\n  \"overall_confidence\": \"low\"\n}",
